The paired result records, generated tables, and comparison plots are produced
by the automated deterministic evaluation. Consequently, the values in
Tables~\ref{tab:multi_observer_scenario_comparison}
and~\ref{tab:multi_observer_aggregate_comparison} are calculated directly from
the observer outputs rather than transcribed into the manuscript.

\subsubsection{Ten-seed OU-family and adaptation study}

\input{w3d-ou-validation-results-generated.tex-part}

\IfFileExists{ou_validation_vertical.svg}{%
  \begin{figure*}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[
      width=0.92\textwidth,
      inkscapelatex=false
    ]{ou_validation_vertical}
    \caption{Ten-seed vertical-displacement RMS means and bootstrap 95\%
    intervals for the adaptive and fixed-tuning variants.  The transition is
    scored over the same final \SI{900}{s} window as the stationary cases.}
    \label{fig:ou_mc_vertical}
  \end{figure*}
}{}

Across the four stationary JONSWAP seas, the mean normalized vertical error
formed by first averaging the four seas within each seed triplet is
$\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIIINormalizedStd\%$ of
$H_s$ for OU--III and
$\OUValidationOUIINormalizedMean\pm\OUValidationOUIINormalizedStd\%$ for
OU--II.  The paired OU--III-minus-OU--II difference is
$\OUValidationNormalizedDifference$ percentage point, with bootstrap 95\%
interval
$[\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceLow,\OUValidationNormalizedDifferenceHigh]$,
Cohen's $d_z=\OUValidationNormalizedDz$, and Hedges' corrected
$g_z=\OUValidationNormalizedGz$ ($n=\OUValidationStationaryPairs$ paired
seed-level aggregates).

That aggregate does not imply uniform dominance.  Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_family}
shows resolved OU--III vertical improvements at $H_s=0.27$ and \SI{4.0}{m}, no
resolved difference at \SI{1.5}{m}, and higher OU--III vertical error at
\SI{8.5}{m} and during the transition.  Moreover, the paired 3D-displacement
difference is positive in every row, with all five bootstrap intervals above
zero.  The additional OU state therefore provides a modest aggregate vertical
benefit for these stationary seas but cannot be described as uniformly better
in either vertical or full three-dimensional reconstruction.  Roll and pitch
effects likewise change sign or have intervals spanning zero in most cases.

The adaptation ablation in Table~\ref{tab:ou_mc_adaptation} is also mixed.
Relative to FixedNominal, Adaptive reduces normalized vertical error at both
off-nominal extremes: by 13.330 and 0.748 percentage points for OU--II and by
9.783 and 1.967 points for OU--III at $H_s=0.27$ and \SI{8.5}{m}, respectively;
all four paired bootstrap intervals exclude zero.  During the controlled
transition, however, Adaptive is worse than FixedNominal by 1.080 points
(95\% interval $[0.880,1.303]$) for OU--II and 1.038 points
($[0.693,1.414]$) for OU--III.  It is also worse than the clairvoyant endpoint
FixedOracle by 1.345 and 2.020 points, respectively.  Online tuning therefore
improves robustness to large stationary mismatch but exhibits transition lag
and does not dominate either fixed reference over the evaluated ramp.

Only \OUValidationLegacyGatePasses{} of the 300 phase-surrogate runs satisfy
all legacy final-\SI{60}{s} thresholds; the remaining
\OUValidationLegacyGateFailures{} trigger at least one.  Those thresholds were
constructed as regression sentinels for the original deterministic realization,
not as population-level acceptance criteria.  The ensemble conclusions are
therefore based on the versioned raw metrics, paired differences, and intervals
rather than on the legacy pass/fail indicator.
